  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 931 AM MDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nThe upper-level low moving across the Four Corners will keep\nconditions dry and breezy today through tomorrow afternoon.\nPrecipitation will be to our east as a trailing front keeps \nmoisture out of our area. Winds today will be from the west-\nsouthwest at 10-20 mph, and gusts up to 30 mph in Sierra County\nand northern Otero County. Winds will shift to the north overnight\ninto tomorrow as a high pressure system spills into the northern \nPlains, decreasing wind speeds to 5-15 mph during the day. \nTemperatures today and through tomorrow afternoon will be at or \naround seasonal averages. These pleasant conditions belie a\ndramatic change ahead.\n\nA second upper-level low will break off from the predominant low\nmoving across the Plains as a ridge starts to dig into the trough\nin the Pacific Northwest. This new low will bring a potent cold\nfront from the northeast Thursday evening, bringing strong\nnortheasterly winds to the region, especially out east. Thursday\nevening through Friday morning will be the riskiest period for\nwinds, as sustained wind speeds from 25 to 35 mph will be felt\nacross the region (though Las Cruces up to Holloman AFB will be in\na pocket of lighter winds at this time). Wind gusts in the High\nWind Watch zones could reach 60 mph or higher. Many of the zones\nin the High Wind Watch will likely be upgraded to a High Wind \nWarning later today, especially for El Paso, Hudspeth, and Otero \nCounties. Other zones like Dona Ana County could see a Wind\nAdvisory at least. Recent rains should keep blowing dust concerns\nlow, but it cannot be ruled out due to the breezy wind gusts.\n\nTemperatures on Friday and Saturday will struggle to even reach \n60F on Friday as the cold front plummets temperatures to 20-25 \ndegrees below average. The Black Range and Sacramento Mountains\ncould feel more like winter than fall on Friday and are expected\nto see their first freeze of the season. As moisture from the\nsecond low pulls in moisture from the south, some high terrain\nlocales may even see a mix of rain and snow early Saturday morning\nif the moisture is advected far enough north. Should any snow fall\nin the high terrain, little to no accumulation is expected due to\nground temperatures being too warm. Outside of the high terrain,\nsome isolated to scattered showers are possible in the morning \nhours on Friday in the eastern reaches of Hudspeth County. Rain \nchances diminish on Saturday and temperatures begin their climb \nback to normal on Sunday as the cutoff low off the Baja coast gets\npicked up by an upper-level trough moving north of us. Another \nPacific low is visible on both the extended GFS and ECMWF runs \nearly next week, which could bring an increase in showers and \nstorms by midweek next week, though confidence in impacts is \ncurrently low.",
